1
00:00:02,669 --> 00:00:05,547
Did you know that in the original Columbia Pictures logo

2
00:00:05,755 --> 00:00:09,092
it was Irene Dunne that is photographed

3
00:00:09,301 --> 00:00:11,219
standing there holding the torch ?

4
00:00:12,554 --> 00:00:16,433
Of course , Columbia Pictures at that time had been bought by Sony .

5
00:00:21,855 --> 00:00:24,399
And we begin the prologue of Dracula

6
00:00:24,608 --> 00:00:29,696
which is intended to provide some historical background of the setting

7
00:00:29,905 --> 00:00:33,200
the unique setting of the story of Vlad Tepes

8
00:00:33,408 --> 00:00:36,870
who was the historical figure of Dracula .

9
00:00:37,913 --> 00:00:40,957
The year , 1462 .

10
00:00:41,166 --> 00:00:44,461
Constantinople had fallen .

11
00:00:44,669 --> 00:00:48,423
Moslem Turks swept into Europe with a vast superior force

12
00:00:48,632 --> 00:00:52,636
Striking at Romania , threatening all of Christendom .

13
00:00:52,844 --> 00:00:55,096
From Transylvania arose a Romanian knight

14
00:00:55,305 --> 00:00:57,557
of the Sacred Order of the Dragon

15
00:00:57,766 --> 00:00:59,351
known as Dracula .

16
00:00:59,559 --> 00:01:04,856
We begin the story with the tale of how the Sultan Mehmed II

17
00:01:05,065 --> 00:01:10,403
finally was able to conquer Constantinople .

18
00:01:10,612 --> 00:01:13,698
And it became the seat of the sultanate .

19
00:01:16,701 --> 00:01:19,496
And of course , all the countries in that area

20
00:01:19,704 --> 00:01:21,790
Romania being one

21
00:01:21,998 --> 00:01:26,086
the Balkans were profoundly affected

22
00:01:26,294 --> 00:01:29,714
by the presence of the Islamic sultan

23
00:01:29,923 --> 00:01:32,384
that they 're so close in Constantinople

24
00:01:32,592 --> 00:01:35,387
which had been the Byzantine empire .

25
00:01:36,346 --> 00:01:40,642
And as Christians , the Romanians were caught between

26
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:44,688
the polarity of this very powerful Ottoman Empire

27
00:01:44,896 --> 00:01:48,233
and the Christian European states , which were quite far away .

28
00:01:48,441 --> 00:01:53,530
So in a sense , the Romanians were left to deal with this problem

29
00:01:53,738 --> 00:01:57,325
and Vlad was a ferocious warrior .

30
00:01:57,534 --> 00:02:01,329
And it was his reputation as the Impaler

31
00:02:01,538 --> 00:02:03,707
that provided psychological warfare

32
00:02:03,915 --> 00:02:07,502
for the superior Turkish Ottoman forces .

33
00:02:09,296 --> 00:02:13,091
This prologue was pretty much created after the fact

34
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:17,178
by my son Roman , who was the second unit director

35
00:02:17,387 --> 00:02:19,556
and did the special effects .

36
00:02:19,764 --> 00:02:22,392
And of course , the problem was , we had very little money left

37
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:26,938
and decided to tell this opening prologue almost as a puppet show

38
00:02:27,147 --> 00:02:30,233
with shadows and banners .

39
00:02:30,442 --> 00:02:33,695
We didn 't have many people or soldiers

40
00:02:33,903 --> 00:02:37,490
and told it in a very abbreviated form

41
00:02:37,699 --> 00:02:39,117
which was storyboarded

42
00:02:39,326 --> 00:02:43,621
and which used the great work of our colleague , Gary Gutierrez

43
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:48,793
who had worked on any number of title and special sequences

44
00:02:49,002 --> 00:02:52,505
and helped us figure out how to tell all of this history

45
00:02:52,714 --> 00:02:54,341
in a way that we could afford .

46
00:02:54,549 --> 00:02:58,887
I mean , using images , in this case , dolls and puppets

47
00:02:59,095 --> 00:03:02,015
and old-fashioned effects concepts

48
00:03:02,223 --> 00:03:06,102
such as glass shots and hanging miniatures .

49
00:03:11,608 --> 00:03:13,234
So when the young actress

50
00:03:13,443 --> 00:03:14,819
Winona Ryder came to see me

51
00:03:15,028 --> 00:03:18,573
and the purpose of our discussion was really

52
00:03:18,782 --> 00:03:20,825
about the fact of how she had dropped out

53
00:03:21,034 --> 00:03:23,328
of working on Godfather III .

54
00:03:23,536 --> 00:03:25,538
Winona was supposed to play the young daughter

55
00:03:25,747 --> 00:03:26,998
of the Godfather II story .

56
00:03:27,207 --> 00:03:29,834
And when she came , she didn 't feel well .

57
00:03:30,043 --> 00:03:32,337
And she basically withdrew from the film

58
00:03:32,545 --> 00:03:35,215
leaving me in a tough spot for Godfather Ill .

59
00:03:35,423 --> 00:03:37,300
Much later we talked about it

60
00:03:37,509 --> 00:03:40,261
and I didn 't want to have a grudge against a young person

61
00:03:40,470 --> 00:03:42,138
So I tried to be nice to her and say :

62
00:03:42,347 --> 00:03:43,848
" Yes , I understand what happened . "

63
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:47,143
And she said , " Well , good , because I have this script of Dracula .

64
00:03:47,352 --> 00:03:48,853
Would you consider doing it ? "

65
00:03:49,062 --> 00:03:51,523
And , of course , that was a magic word to me

66
00:03:51,731 --> 00:03:53,817
because I not only knew the book so well

67
00:03:54,025 --> 00:03:56,861
from this experience reading it to my little boys

68
00:03:57,070 --> 00:04:00,240
but also I knew about the historical figure of Dracula

69
00:04:00,448 --> 00:04:05,078
who was Dracula , meaning the son of Dracul .

70
00:04:05,286 --> 00:04:07,372
And Dracul was the Order of the Dragon

71
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:11,000
which was given to certain Romanian noblemen

72
00:04:11,209 --> 00:04:15,547
because of their service in the very difficult situation Romania was in

73
00:04:15,755 --> 00:04:20,135
being between the great Ottoman Empire , which was so close to it

74
00:04:20,343 --> 00:04:25,306
and yet being Christian , and being , really , knights to defend Christianity .

75
00:04:25,515 --> 00:04:31,729
And so this historical person , Dracula , who was really known as Vlad Tepes - -

76
00:04:31,938 --> 00:04:36,151
So I knew a little bit about this historical king of Romania

77
00:04:36,359 --> 00:04:39,070
and I knew the story of how Bram Stoker

78
00:04:39,279 --> 00:04:43,992
had appropriated this history to combine it into a vampire tale .

79
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:45,994
So I agreed to do it .

80
00:04:46,202 --> 00:04:49,789
It was a time when I was really sort of putting my life together

81
00:04:49,998 --> 00:04:53,918
after some of the big financial setbacks that I had had

82
00:04:54,127 --> 00:04:57,630
and which was what led me to make the third Godfather

83
00:04:57,839 --> 00:05:01,009
and the Dracula picture , and kind of stabilize my life

84
00:05:01,217 --> 00:05:04,095
at a time when it had been pretty rocky .

85
00:05:16,149 --> 00:05:17,650
The costume designer

86
00:05:17,859 --> 00:05:24,616
Eiko Ishioka , was given the challenge to try to find a new kind of imagery .

87
00:05:24,824 --> 00:05:28,578
As example , his unusual armor .

88
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:39,506
This title was done , as was much of this whole prologue

89
00:05:39,714 --> 00:05:42,550
by Roman and by our colleague Gary Gutierrez

90
00:05:42,759 --> 00:05:44,260
a San Francisco filmmaker

91
00:05:44,469 --> 00:05:45,178
who worked on many , many Zoetrope films

92
00:05:45,178 --> 00:05:48,640
who worked on many , many Zoetrope films

93
00:05:48,848 --> 00:05:51,768
and was an Academy Award winner

94
00:05:51,976 --> 00:05:54,771
for Phil Kaufman 's film The Right Stuff .

95
00:06:01,069 --> 00:06:03,696
I 've done everything that you asked , master .

96
00:06:03,905 --> 00:06:10,078
Tom Waits played Renfield , who was , like , the former real-estate man

97
00:06:10,286 --> 00:06:14,457
who had gone off to Transylvania to attempt

98
00:06:14,666 --> 00:06:17,877
to do a real-estate deal with this mysterious Count Dracula

99
00:06:18,086 --> 00:06:20,463
as it 's told in the Bram Stoker book .

100
00:06:20,672 --> 00:06:23,675
The Bram Stoker novel is written in an unusual way .

101
00:06:23,883 --> 00:06:28,638
It 's written as a series of entries , notes , diary entries

102
00:06:28,846 --> 00:06:31,432
or other documents , all in a pastiche

103
00:06:31,641 --> 00:06:33,226
to move the story forward

104
00:06:33,434 --> 00:06:36,563
which is basically the story of a young solicitor

105
00:06:36,771 --> 00:06:40,692
or I think he was , like , a real-estate agent , as played by Keanu Reeves

106
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:44,362
Jonathan Harker , who is given the job by his firm

107
00:06:44,571 --> 00:06:50,493
to go to the distant East European country of Romania

108
00:06:50,702 --> 00:06:53,830
to that region of it which is called Transylvania

109
00:06:54,038 --> 00:06:58,585
which technically was under the influence of the Hungarian empire

110
00:06:58,793 --> 00:07:01,546
to conclude a business deal with Count Dracula .

111
00:07:01,754 --> 00:07:04,424
He wished to buy some real estate in England .

112
00:07:05,174 --> 00:07:08,052
what in fact happened to Mr. Renfield in Transylvania ?

113
00:07:09,178 --> 00:07:13,349
Nothing . Nothing . Personal problems .

114
00:07:13,558 --> 00:07:18,229
Close these transactions , and your future with this firm is assured .

115
00:07:18,438 --> 00:07:19,480
Yes , Sir ...

116
00:07:19,689 --> 00:07:23,484
So Harker is sent on his way

117
00:07:23,693 --> 00:07:26,237
to make this trip by train to conclude it .

118
00:07:26,446 --> 00:07:31,618
Of course , before going , he bids adieu to his sweetheart , his fiancé

119
00:07:31,826 --> 00:07:34,037
as played by Winona Ryder .

120
00:07:34,245 --> 00:07:35,913
Of course , they 're about to be married

121
00:07:36,122 --> 00:07:39,959
and she 's very reluctant that he go away from her .

122
00:07:41,044 --> 00:07:42,712
All of these settings

123
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:47,967
were done at the sound stages , formerly MGM , in fact .

124
00:07:48,176 --> 00:07:52,930
This was a great big sound stage that had a pool .

125
00:07:53,139 --> 00:07:56,559
And this was the pool where Esther Williams made all of her films

126
00:07:56,768 --> 00:07:58,770
in the MGM era .

127
00:08:03,107 --> 00:08:08,196
We begin now something that is part of the style of the picture

128
00:08:08,404 --> 00:08:12,283
in that we pre-visualized , or pre-designed

129
00:08:12,492 --> 00:08:14,202
how everything would work .

130
00:08:14,410 --> 00:08:17,830
And my son Roman worked extensively on this

131
00:08:18,039 --> 00:08:20,500
and shot many of these sequences .

132
00:08:20,708 --> 00:08:24,379
These scenes you are seeing , with the train going over the diary

133
00:08:24,587 --> 00:08:28,549
and the eyes and face of Dracula in the windows

134
00:08:28,758 --> 00:08:30,218
were all done in-camera

135
00:08:30,426 --> 00:08:35,723
meaning that the shot of him by the window was shot

136
00:08:35,932 --> 00:08:37,809
and then the film was wound by hand .

137
00:08:38,017 --> 00:08:41,979
In this case , the image of the map is projected on his face .

138
00:08:42,188 --> 00:08:45,274
Everything is live . It 's not done in post-production

139
00:08:45,483 --> 00:08:47,485
as it would be done in the modern times .

140
00:08:47,694 --> 00:08:50,530
This plate that you see in the window

141
00:08:50,738 --> 00:08:53,533
with the landscape of the Carpathian Mountains

142
00:08:53,741 --> 00:08:56,285
and the eyes of the mysterious count

143
00:08:56,494 --> 00:09:00,123
are all being combined in the film .

144
00:09:00,331 --> 00:09:05,878
It 's an effect not unlike the great German silent director G. W. Pabst

145
00:09:06,087 --> 00:09:10,133
or Murnau , who made Nosferatu , would have done .

146
00:09:12,135 --> 00:09:15,847
Your friend , D.

147
00:09:16,472 --> 00:09:20,017
It 's interesting , I see the letter , and he says , " Your friend , D. "

148
00:09:20,226 --> 00:09:23,020
For a while , I was suggesting that we perhaps

149
00:09:23,229 --> 00:09:25,690
just call the movie D. with a period

150
00:09:25,898 --> 00:09:29,402
just to try to designate it as being a little different

151
00:09:29,610 --> 00:09:33,781
from the more familiar Dracula movies .

152
00:09:33,990 --> 00:09:36,159
But I guess that wasn 't such a good idea .

153
00:09:36,367 --> 00:09:39,996
At any rate , it wasn 't an idea that was used .

154
00:09:41,205 --> 00:09:45,168
It must be so nice to see strange countries .

155
00:09:45,376 --> 00:09:47,211
I wonder if we , I mean Jonathan and I ...

156
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:49,964
The script , written by Jim Hart , tried to follow - -

157
00:09:50,173 --> 00:09:53,468
And I agreed very much to follow the story

158
00:09:53,676 --> 00:09:55,052
as it was in the book .

159
00:09:55,261 --> 00:09:58,681
To make a version of Dracula that was very much like the book .

160
00:09:58,890 --> 00:10:00,850
Not only did I know the book well - -

161
00:10:01,058 --> 00:10:07,648
I used to read it to my little camp students when I was a camp counselor .

162
00:10:07,857 --> 00:10:11,194
- - But also , I had the entire cast sit around for probably three days

163
00:10:11,402 --> 00:10:12,779
and read the book aloud

164
00:10:12,987 --> 00:10:16,157
something that really frustrated Anthony Hopkins .

165
00:10:16,365 --> 00:10:18,367
Didn 't see for the life of him

166
00:10:18,576 --> 00:10:21,662
why I wanted to have them read the entire book .

167
00:10:21,871 --> 00:10:23,414
And of course , I did

168
00:10:23,623 --> 00:10:25,750
because I wanted to be sure they all read the book .

169
00:10:25,958 --> 00:10:28,461
And also , I was hoping we 'd discover something in the book

170
00:10:28,669 --> 00:10:29,962
that had been left out .

171
00:10:37,470 --> 00:10:38,805
We did work with wolves .

172
00:10:39,013 --> 00:10:42,308
They were real wolves , and they 're tricky to work with .

173
00:10:42,517 --> 00:10:45,937
You have to be very respectful of their territory .

174
00:10:47,688 --> 00:10:51,984
The scene where the ghostly driver of the coach picks up Harker

175
00:10:52,193 --> 00:10:53,820
was a problem for me at first

176
00:10:54,028 --> 00:10:56,239
because I remember the book described

177
00:10:56,447 --> 00:11:00,618
how the coachman just reached his hand over to Harker

178
00:11:00,827 --> 00:11:03,830
and the hand just seemed to extend so far

179
00:11:04,038 --> 00:11:06,874
and literally pick him up and lift him up into the coach .

180
00:11:07,083 --> 00:11:10,711
And there was a lot of concern of how we could do this as a live-action

181
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:14,590
meaning this really was photographed as it was happening .

182
00:11:14,799 --> 00:11:18,302
And the special-effect man went home

183
00:11:18,511 --> 00:11:20,471
when we failed to know how to do it

184
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:23,432
and came back with a solution that was quite interesting

185
00:11:23,641 --> 00:11:28,354
involving the coachman and Harker standing on boards of wood

186
00:11:28,563 --> 00:11:33,317
that were leveraged in such a way that we could actually

187
00:11:33,526 --> 00:11:38,698
move the coachman to reach for him , and move them into the room .

188
00:11:38,906 --> 00:11:40,950
This shot is a glass shot

189
00:11:41,158 --> 00:11:46,080
which means part of the scene is painted on a piece of glass

190
00:11:46,289 --> 00:11:49,876
and then the real coach , which is in a sound stage

191
00:11:50,084 --> 00:11:53,588
Is shot through that glass , or in conjunction with that glass

192
00:11:53,796 --> 00:11:57,592
10 enable it to look as though you 're seeing the whole image .

193
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:01,137
This was a very common way to do effects

194
00:12:01,345 --> 00:12:03,890
In the golden age of movies

195
00:12:04,098 --> 00:12:06,392
before these things could be accomplished

196
00:12:06,601 --> 00:12:08,686
with digital and other effects .

197
00:12:18,404 --> 00:12:22,074
Much of this movie really is a theatrical illusion .

198
00:12:23,826 --> 00:12:27,622
Which was my goal . I wanted to find a new way

199
00:12:27,830 --> 00:12:28,372
to find imagery for the story .

200
00:12:28,372 --> 00:12:30,791
... to find imagery for the story .

201
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,962
And I had this fabulous studio to work with .

202
00:12:35,171 --> 00:12:40,426
Already , you can see one of the themes of the visualization

203
00:12:40,635 --> 00:12:44,221
of the movie , which is the use of shadows .

204
00:12:44,430 --> 00:12:51,354
It was my idea that in the presence of a metaphysical phenomenon

205
00:12:51,562 --> 00:12:55,900
such as a vampire , that the laws of nature , of physics

206
00:12:56,108 --> 00:12:57,777
don 't really work correctly .

207
00:12:57,985 --> 00:13:03,282
Shadows seem to be liberated from the person casting them .

208
00:13:03,491 --> 00:13:06,619
Things fall up instead of fall down .

209
00:13:06,827 --> 00:13:11,791
That you know you 're in a realm of supernatural , because subtly

210
00:13:11,999 --> 00:13:14,168
things don 't happen correctly .

211
00:13:14,377 --> 00:13:20,007
Now , it 's well-known that once you step into the world of a vampire

212
00:13:20,216 --> 00:13:24,553
you cannot be harmed unless you voluntarily take that step yourself .

213
00:13:24,762 --> 00:13:30,101
And so when Harker takes those first steps over , across the threshold

214
00:13:30,309 --> 00:13:34,689
he has willingly put himself into the realm of that vampire

215
00:13:34,897 --> 00:13:36,774
and thus is in danger .

216
00:13:38,067 --> 00:13:41,821
I was also told that once by Mario Puzo , in regard to the Mafia .

217
00:13:42,029 --> 00:13:46,200
That the Mafia would be - - People who were truly in the Mafia - -

218
00:13:46,409 --> 00:13:51,080
He had never met anyone that was actually associated in the Mafia .

219
00:13:51,288 --> 00:13:55,126
And he advised me never to willingly know them

220
00:13:55,334 --> 00:13:57,586
to let them know your phone number

221
00:13:57,795 --> 00:13:59,755
or that you would be friendly with them .

222
00:13:59,964 --> 00:14:04,260
And I often thought that the same rule was the rule of the vampire

223
00:14:04,468 --> 00:14:06,804
that when dealing with the Mafia

224
00:14:07,013 --> 00:14:09,598
if you did not invite them into your life

225
00:14:09,807 --> 00:14:13,894
by way of accepting their friendship , or a gift - -

226
00:14:14,103 --> 00:14:16,022
Which was probably stolen , by the way .

227
00:14:16,230 --> 00:14:18,274
- - Or go to one of their good restaurants

228
00:14:18,482 --> 00:14:20,568
that they could never have any - -

229
00:14:20,776 --> 00:14:24,280
They would respectfully not intrude in your life .

230
00:14:24,488 --> 00:14:27,283
And I always followed that rule given by Mario

231
00:14:27,491 --> 00:14:29,618
and therefore , I never had any encounter

232
00:14:29,827 --> 00:14:32,538
or any knowledge of any Mafia person

233
00:14:32,747 --> 00:14:37,334
despite having made a film that was well-known about that group of people .

234
00:14:37,543 --> 00:14:39,045
And by the same token

235
00:14:39,253 --> 00:14:43,883
had Jonathan Harker not voluntarily walked over

236
00:14:44,091 --> 00:14:46,510
Dracula 's threshold

237
00:14:46,719 --> 00:14:49,263
he would not have been in danger .

238
00:14:54,101 --> 00:14:55,811
Blood ...

239
00:14:56,062 --> 00:15:02,359
Of course , this performance of Gary Oldman

240
00:15:02,568 --> 00:15:05,029
attempted to blaze a new trail

241
00:15:05,237 --> 00:15:08,449
making use of the historical Vlad Tepes

242
00:15:08,657 --> 00:15:11,368
the picture of which is on the portrait .

243
00:15:11,577 --> 00:15:15,081
And as well as a character

244
00:15:15,289 --> 00:15:17,458
the eccentric count living in an old castle

245
00:15:17,666 --> 00:15:20,086
that had been made so famous by Bela Lugosi .

246
00:15:20,294 --> 00:15:24,048
And we felt very much that we were gonna go in another direction

247
00:15:24,256 --> 00:15:28,552
for better or for worse , and try to find a new kind of imagery .

248
00:15:28,761 --> 00:15:32,848
And part of this was , we were going to have costumes

249
00:15:33,057 --> 00:15:35,059
and imagery that was totally different .

250
00:15:35,267 --> 00:15:36,519
Forgive me .

251
00:15:38,020 --> 00:15:39,814
Another interesting example

252
00:15:40,022 --> 00:15:42,733
of the detail that my son Roman did .

