992
01:03:40,483 --> 01:03:45,446
And I liked having the chance to do all these wacky ideas

993
01:03:45,655 --> 01:03:49,492
and I remember being very disgruntled over the fact

994
01:03:49,701 --> 01:03:53,871
that I had tried so hard , and indeed executed very hard

995
01:03:54,080 --> 01:03:57,917
doing it exactly on schedule and on budget .

996
01:03:58,126 --> 01:04:01,504
And so much to the point that I remember going to the executives

997
01:04:01,713 --> 01:04:05,800
and saying , " Look , we 're now a week ahead of schedule

998
01:04:06,009 --> 01:04:09,262
and could I have the money that that week represents

999
01:04:09,470 --> 01:04:12,473
and spend it on additional special effects ? "

1000
01:04:12,682 --> 01:04:17,603
And they studied it and said , " Indeed , you are ahead of the budget

1001
01:04:17,812 --> 01:04:20,857
and so you can have the money for these extra special effects . "

1002
01:04:21,065 --> 01:04:23,901
And then about two weeks later , I get this visit

1003
01:04:24,110 --> 01:04:27,822
from this obnoxious studio executive who tells me

1004
01:04:28,031 --> 01:04:31,868
that they 've just gotten a bill of all people from the studio

1005
01:04:32,076 --> 01:04:33,828
which is to say , from them

1006
01:04:34,037 --> 01:04:37,373
and somehow , their accounting system was broken

1007
01:04:37,582 --> 01:04:40,209
but there were many , many more light rentals

1008
01:04:40,418 --> 01:04:44,380
and equipment rentals that anyone knew about .

1009
01:04:44,589 --> 01:04:47,133
And it really , you know , made me feel frustrated .

1010
01:04:47,341 --> 01:04:50,053
And I had tried and succeeded to make this big production

1011
01:04:50,261 --> 01:04:53,306
on , you know , what was not a super-affluent budget

1012
01:04:53,514 --> 01:04:55,141
but certainly a respectable budget .

1013
01:04:55,349 --> 01:04:57,143
And I had done it so carefully

1014
01:04:57,351 --> 01:04:59,896
and even to the point that they themselves agreed

1015
01:05:00,104 --> 01:05:01,564
that I was ahead of budget .

1016
01:05:01,773 --> 01:05:05,902
And then to be told that , oh , their billings were screwed up

1017
01:05:06,110 --> 01:05:08,738
and therefore , I was suddenly , magically over budget

1018
01:05:08,946 --> 01:05:11,365
so that they could do what they like to do so much

1019
01:05:11,574 --> 01:05:14,035
which is to come in and castigate you , and ...

1020
01:05:15,495 --> 01:05:17,747
This sequence , by the way , on a more pleasant subject

1021
01:05:17,955 --> 01:05:19,540
was the absinthe sequence .

1022
01:05:19,749 --> 01:05:23,461
And Roman did such beautiful effects work in it

1023
01:05:23,669 --> 01:05:27,924
10 weave the legendary and somewhat magical effects

1024
01:05:28,132 --> 01:05:30,176
of the drink absinthe

1025
01:05:30,384 --> 01:05:33,805
which was , of course , made illegal around this time

1026
01:05:34,013 --> 01:05:38,267
or a little later , primarily because it was the first aperitif

1027
01:05:38,476 --> 01:05:40,812
that was made out of something other than grapes .

1028
01:05:41,020 --> 01:05:44,941
And all the grape owners and makers of aperitifs

1029
01:05:45,149 --> 01:05:46,442
which came from grapes

1030
01:05:46,651 --> 01:05:51,155
really got together and got it banned for its dangerous effects .

1031
01:05:51,364 --> 01:05:54,450
Notably because of a murder that happened in France

1032
01:05:54,659 --> 01:05:57,787
where some man drank some absinthe and then got crazy

1033
01:05:57,995 --> 01:05:59,580
went home and murdered his family .

1034
01:05:59,789 --> 01:06:02,041
But no one had said that before he drank the absinthe

1035
01:06:02,250 --> 01:06:05,211
he had drunk two quarts of brandy

1036
01:06:05,419 --> 01:06:09,674
and sniffed so many tons of various other drugs .

1037
01:06:09,882 --> 01:06:15,763
And so absinthe was made illegal , probably quite unfairly

1038
01:06:15,972 --> 01:06:22,019
because it basically threatened what was the reigning business

1039
01:06:22,228 --> 01:06:24,897
which was making aperitifs out of grapes .

1040
01:06:25,106 --> 01:06:27,525
But we tried to have a love scene

1041
01:06:27,733 --> 01:06:32,905
with imagery liberated by the thought that they 're drinking absinthe .

1042
01:06:33,114 --> 01:06:35,741
As with every scene , there was a new challenge

1043
01:06:35,950 --> 01:06:37,785
of how can we show the past

1044
01:06:37,994 --> 01:06:42,290
show her resonance with the ancient woman that he had loved

1045
01:06:42,498 --> 01:06:46,836
and , again , present Dracula as a romantic figure

1046
01:06:47,044 --> 01:06:48,921
rather than just a monster ?

1047
01:06:49,714 --> 01:06:51,507
The princess

1048
01:06:52,592 --> 01:06:53,634
she 's a river ...

1049
01:06:53,843 --> 01:06:56,262
I think this movie

1050
01:06:56,470 --> 01:06:58,681
you know , was thought of many different ways .

1051
01:06:58,890 --> 01:07:01,517
But among the most enthusiastic people

1052
01:07:01,726 --> 01:07:04,812
who seem to have enjoyed the film are women .

1053
01:07:05,021 --> 01:07:08,399
It 's that - - I guess the nature of the love story .

1054
01:07:08,608 --> 01:07:10,067
I mean , everybody at some point

1055
01:07:10,276 --> 01:07:13,571
Is in love with someone who 's bad for them .

1056
01:07:13,779 --> 01:07:17,033
Certainly , being in love with Dracula would fit that description .

1057
01:07:22,663 --> 01:07:26,792
I thought , at the time , Winona Ryder was a very talented young lady .

1058
01:07:27,001 --> 01:07:30,213
And like so many people who start when they 're really young

1059
01:07:30,421 --> 01:07:32,298
they just have a gift .

1060
01:07:32,506 --> 01:07:35,885
But one of the possible problems with that

1061
01:07:36,093 --> 01:07:39,013
Is that they tend to not go all the way .

1062
01:07:39,222 --> 01:07:43,643
There comes a point where they know so much , it almost works against them .

1063
01:07:43,851 --> 01:07:45,811
But I will not be surprised

1064
01:07:46,020 --> 01:07:49,857
if Winona suddenly is in some movie at any time

1065
01:07:50,066 --> 01:07:53,236
and just astonishes everyone .

1066
01:07:55,321 --> 01:07:59,242
This is definitely an homage to Cocteau

1067
01:07:59,450 --> 01:08:00,576
but a lovely image .

1068
01:08:00,785 --> 01:08:04,830
He takes her tears and gives them to her as diamonds .

1069
01:08:05,039 --> 01:08:08,793
Every man would like to do that , if he makes the woman he loves cry .

1070
01:08:14,590 --> 01:08:16,342
Choosing a composer for a movie

1071
01:08:16,550 --> 01:08:20,221
Is always a very important thing in any project .

1072
01:08:20,429 --> 01:08:23,891
But for me in particular , being the son of a composer

1073
01:08:24,100 --> 01:08:27,395
being raised in a musical household

1074
01:08:27,603 --> 01:08:30,606
I very much wanted a classical score .

1075
01:08:30,815 --> 01:08:35,695
And my knowledge told me that some of the greatest classical composers

1076
01:08:35,903 --> 01:08:39,991
were in the East European world , particularly in Poland

1077
01:08:40,199 --> 01:08:45,204
partly to do with the state sponsorship of classical music .

1078
01:08:45,413 --> 01:08:49,125
And for a long time , the composer I really wanted to do it

1079
01:08:49,333 --> 01:08:53,963
was a great Polish composer named Lutoslawski .

1080
01:08:54,171 --> 01:08:57,383
I eventually tracked him down at a concert

1081
01:08:57,591 --> 01:08:59,302
where he was conducting his own work .

1082
01:08:59,510 --> 01:09:03,639
And when I proposed that he might consider doing a score for me

1083
01:09:03,848 --> 01:09:07,393
his reaction was sort of like at the level of , I mean :

1084
01:09:07,601 --> 01:09:12,023
" Young man , do you know how many hours it takes me

1085
01:09:12,231 --> 01:09:14,191
to write one minute of music ? "

1086
01:09:14,400 --> 01:09:19,572
And that - - The implication being that , at that level of classical music - -

1087
01:09:19,780 --> 01:09:23,492
And it 's not only the composition , it 's the orchestration .

1088
01:09:23,701 --> 01:09:27,788
- - It is a very arduous , time-consuming job

1089
01:09:27,997 --> 01:09:30,791
to compose so many minutes of music .

1090
01:09:31,000 --> 01:09:33,794
It 's not as we think of it , where , oh , you get a motif

1091
01:09:34,003 --> 01:09:36,297
and possibly bring in an orchestrator .

1092
01:09:36,505 --> 01:09:38,257
And you play the theme over and over again .

1093
01:09:38,466 --> 01:09:41,594
So at that level of handmade music

1094
01:09:41,802 --> 01:09:45,556
it 's incredibly time-consuming .

1095
01:09:45,765 --> 01:09:48,476
And basically , what he was saying is , you know

1096
01:09:48,684 --> 01:09:52,271
he wouldn 't be able to devote the time to write whatever it is

1097
01:09:52,480 --> 01:09:56,317
30 minutes , 40 minutes of new music .

1098
01:09:56,525 --> 01:10:01,822
And actually , in truth , he died not that long after that brief meeting .

1099
01:10:02,031 --> 01:10:05,743
But I continued researching Polish composers .

1100
01:10:05,951 --> 01:10:10,790
And I was very interested in Ligeti , Gorecki , many wonderful composers

1101
01:10:10,998 --> 01:10:13,959
some of which I heard in Stanley Kubrick 's last film .

1102
01:10:14,168 --> 01:10:16,087
Although it was not an original composition

1103
01:10:16,295 --> 01:10:19,548
I believe he used existing work of those composers .

1104
01:10:19,757 --> 01:10:26,055
But ultimately , I was led to consider the notion of Kilar

1105
01:10:26,263 --> 01:10:29,892
another Polish composer , to write this score .

1106
01:10:30,101 --> 01:10:32,228
And I approached him , and he was willing .

1107
01:10:32,436 --> 01:10:35,189
And what I noticed from that experience - -

1108
01:10:35,398 --> 01:10:37,733
What he wrote was very , very effective

1109
01:10:37,942 --> 01:10:43,280
and , in fact , really has weathered time very well .

1110
01:10:43,489 --> 01:10:47,660
And for years , I would see trailers of movies coming out

1111
01:10:47,868 --> 01:10:52,832
that had just used his music for Dracula in the trailer .

1112
01:10:53,040 --> 01:10:58,212
And what I noticed was he wrote about three , or at the most four cues .

1113
01:10:58,421 --> 01:10:59,463
I think it was three .

1114
01:10:59,672 --> 01:11:04,093
A love theme , a kind of initial very dramatic theme

1115
01:11:04,301 --> 01:11:06,220
and then a third theme .

1116
01:11:06,429 --> 01:11:09,056
And he gave me these three themes , and we recorded them

1117
01:11:09,265 --> 01:11:11,851
and that was all he gave me .

1118
01:11:12,059 --> 01:11:14,353
And we had a whole movie that we had to score .

1119
01:11:14,562 --> 01:11:15,855
And we had these three themes .

1120
01:11:16,063 --> 01:11:18,274
And I said , " Well , can you give me some variations ?

1121
01:11:18,482 --> 01:11:21,527
For example , could we play the love theme this way and that way

1122
01:11:21,735 --> 01:11:23,571
this orchestration , that orchestration ? "

1123
01:11:23,779 --> 01:11:25,197
And he tried and tried and tried

1124
01:11:25,406 --> 01:11:27,575
but ultimately , when it was all said and done

1125
01:11:27,783 --> 01:11:30,244
he wrote those three cues , they sounded great

1126
01:11:30,453 --> 01:11:32,663
and he was giving it to me , and that was it .

1127
01:11:32,872 --> 01:11:36,083
So when we actually finished the complete movie

1128
01:11:36,292 --> 01:11:38,961
we found that we had to take these three cues , and ...

1129
01:11:39,170 --> 01:11:42,214
Fortunately , we had the original recordings

1130
01:11:42,423 --> 01:11:45,050
which were recorded with a number of microphones .

1131
01:11:45,259 --> 01:11:49,472
So we could play the cue , and , you know , not play the string section

1132
01:11:49,680 --> 01:11:51,348
or not play the brass section

1133
01:11:51,557 --> 01:11:56,770
or the music editor had to find ways that we could , like

1134
01:11:56,979 --> 01:12:01,108
make these three very , very impressive cues

1135
01:12:01,317 --> 01:12:04,612
work for the whole movie without just repeating them over and over again .

1136
01:12:04,820 --> 01:12:07,990
And that was an interesting experience , very , very positive

1137
01:12:08,199 --> 01:12:09,783
and yet somewhat troublesome

1138
01:12:09,992 --> 01:12:14,246
with , like , a really classical musician-composer .

1139
01:12:14,455 --> 01:12:17,041
I guess it comes down to that , you know

1140
01:12:17,249 --> 01:12:20,294
every minute of music is the result of a lot of hard work .

1141
01:12:20,503 --> 01:12:21,837
Weeks and weeks of hard work .

1142
01:12:22,046 --> 01:12:25,132
And you just don 't have , you know , 12 cues

1143
01:12:25,341 --> 01:12:28,844
and do it this way and do it that way , and have enough for a whole movie .

1144
01:12:44,318 --> 01:12:45,945
You know , in a sense , it was - -

1145
01:12:46,153 --> 01:12:49,490
This group of young people , all the people in this scene - -

1146
01:12:49,698 --> 01:12:52,409
It was like being with your kids .

1147
01:12:52,618 --> 01:12:56,789
And for all the plus and minus of that .

1148
01:12:56,997 --> 01:12:59,542
You know , they were all young

1149
01:13:01,502 --> 01:13:03,462
and talented .

1150
01:13:03,754 --> 01:13:05,673
And , you know , I love to work with young people .

1151
01:13:05,881 --> 01:13:08,425
I always did , ever since I was a young person .

1152
01:13:08,801 --> 01:13:11,011
put you out of your misery like a lame horse .

1153
01:13:11,220 --> 01:13:13,138
Quincey .

1154
01:13:14,223 --> 01:13:15,849
You 're such a beast .

1155
01:13:16,058 --> 01:13:19,603
She 's sort of sexy with those cute little baby vampire teeth .

1156
01:13:19,812 --> 01:13:21,689
Will you kiss me , Quincey ?

1157
01:13:23,649 --> 01:13:27,987
Anthony Hopkins was , you know , a fine actor .

1158
01:13:28,195 --> 01:13:32,658
He didn 't like to rehearse .

1159
01:13:32,866 --> 01:13:34,952
And I understand it now , I didn 't then .

1160
01:13:35,160 --> 01:13:38,622
Because I thought all theater actors , they loved to have three , four weeks

1161
01:13:38,831 --> 01:13:40,958
screw around and kind of explore things .

1162
01:13:41,166 --> 01:13:42,876
Do it this way , do it that way .

1163
01:13:43,085 --> 01:13:49,925
But his disposition was such that he liked , you know - -

1164
01:13:50,134 --> 01:13:52,094
If there was no rehearsal , it 'd be fine with him .

1165
01:13:52,303 --> 01:13:55,472
Just come in and start to do it , do your job , and go home .

1166
01:13:55,681 --> 01:14:00,936
And so I really started off at the wrong foot with him

1167
01:14:01,145 --> 01:14:04,565
because I had a very lengthy rehearsal .

1168
01:14:06,275 --> 01:14:08,235
But I found him very cooperative .

1169
01:14:08,444 --> 01:14:11,447
You know , he was ultimately enthusiastic

1170
01:14:11,655 --> 01:14:14,491
and sort of impatient with it all .

1171
01:14:14,700 --> 01:14:21,081
And I don 't imagine that it was one of his favorite movies to work on .

1172
01:14:22,207 --> 01:14:28,088
And I think partly I took too much time in the rehearsal period

1173
01:14:28,297 --> 01:14:33,135
which I guess I was used to because I was a theater director

1174
01:14:33,344 --> 01:14:36,472
or trained in that way , and didn 't know any other way .

1175
01:14:36,680 --> 01:14:40,059
But now the older I get , I think , you know

1176
01:14:40,267 --> 01:14:41,894
maybe there 's something to speak - -

1177
01:14:42,102 --> 01:14:44,521
Because when you actually start to shoot , and you have the - -

1178
01:14:44,730 --> 01:14:47,524
You 're ready , you have the camera , the costumes , and what have you

1179
01:14:47,733 --> 01:14:50,694
things happen that you almost can 't rehearse

1180
01:14:50,903 --> 01:14:53,238
Or you can 't guess are gonna happen .

1181
01:14:53,447 --> 01:14:56,825
And then , you know , as I say , it 's only what you 've gathered .

1182
01:14:57,034 --> 01:14:59,203
It 's only , you went out picking mushrooms

1183
01:14:59,411 --> 01:15:02,164
and that day you got so many mushrooms .

1184
01:15:02,373 --> 01:15:04,124
And later on , when you cut the film

1185
01:15:04,333 --> 01:15:09,088
you 'll see whether or not they were useful .

1186
01:15:09,296 --> 01:15:12,091
You 'll make the movie out of what you have .

1187
01:15:15,844 --> 01:15:18,180
This scene is an example of minimalism .

1188
01:15:18,389 --> 01:15:23,102
The set is just basically the light and the rope .

1189
01:15:23,310 --> 01:15:25,729
And you get the idea she 's on a boat .

1190
01:15:25,938 --> 01:15:29,024
Of course , there 's nothing there but the light and the rope .

1191
01:15:29,233 --> 01:15:33,070
And , you know , of course I love that tradition .

1192
01:15:33,278 --> 01:15:37,366
And you saw much of it over the great golden age of Hollywood .

1193
01:15:37,574 --> 01:15:41,912
The MGM films and Warner Bros. films

1194
01:15:42,121 --> 01:15:48,001
where they were able to give you , by illusion , something

1195
01:15:48,210 --> 01:15:49,878
without having to build a whole set .

1196
01:15:50,087 --> 01:15:53,132
And , of course , it enables you

1197
01:15:53,340 --> 01:15:56,218
to spend more money on something , really , where you have to .

1198
01:15:56,427 --> 01:16:00,639
And you save the money in that or in this case

1199
01:16:00,848 --> 01:16:03,267
where it 's just some candles .

1200
01:16:03,475 --> 01:16:08,856
This sequence with the candles is very much the brainchild of - -

1201
01:16:09,064 --> 01:16:11,650
You know , the idea of having them dancing

1202
01:16:11,859 --> 01:16:13,652
In the candles , and what have you

1203
01:16:13,861 --> 01:16:16,405
was very much the brainchild of Michael Ballhaus

1204
01:16:16,613 --> 01:16:18,240
who was a wonderful man

1205
01:16:18,449 --> 01:16:23,662
and really trying to get with this , what ultimately he understood

1206
01:16:23,871 --> 01:16:28,792
was a kind of far-out style , in which almost anything goes .

1207
01:16:30,711 --> 01:16:34,006
And I think , if I 'm correct

1208
01:16:34,214 --> 01:16:35,758
he dreamt up the idea of a scene

1209
01:16:35,966 --> 01:16:38,927
where there were just some candles and what have you .

1210
01:16:39,344 --> 01:16:41,138
Guard her well , Mr. Morris .

1211
01:16:41,346 --> 01:16:43,724
Do not fail here tonight .

1212
01:16:44,433 --> 01:16:48,187
I remember pushing Van Helsing

1213
01:16:48,395 --> 01:16:52,357
in this direction that you see in this scene .

1214
01:16:52,566 --> 01:16:56,737
That , you know , having him really come on a little , you know ...

1215
01:16:56,945 --> 01:16:58,280
Well , you 're a sick old buzzard .

1216
01:16:58,489 --> 01:16:59,615
... hysterically , you know ?

1217
01:16:59,823 --> 01:17:02,284
And , you know , he 's a great actor .

1218
01:17:02,493 --> 01:17:03,786
And , you know , you could say :

1219
01:17:03,994 --> 01:17:06,789
" Oh , I encouraged him to be a little over-the-top . "

1220
01:17:06,997 --> 01:17:08,999
But , you know , the whole movie is over-the-top .

1221
01:17:09,208 --> 01:17:11,710
So , what 's over-the-top , you know ?

1222
01:17:11,919 --> 01:17:16,131
The top is determined by what the stylistic approach - -

1223
01:17:16,340 --> 01:17:18,175
And after all , we 're making Dracula here .

1224
01:17:18,383 --> 01:17:23,138
This is - - It 's clearly not the first time it was being done .

1225
01:17:23,347 --> 01:17:28,977
As I said , I think Dracula 's probably the most oft-sequelized film on earth .

1226
01:17:29,186 --> 01:17:31,230
And , you know , I don 't get it .

1227
01:17:31,438 --> 01:17:34,441
I didn 't think my job was to come in here

1228
01:17:34,650 --> 01:17:37,027
and do the same thing over again .

1229
01:17:37,236 --> 01:17:40,489
And so , you know , I took the path

1230
01:17:40,697 --> 01:17:44,743
that was really out of my heritage

1231
01:17:44,952 --> 01:17:47,287
and my background and my training

1232
01:17:47,496 --> 01:17:51,041
and my view of things .

1233
01:17:51,416 --> 01:17:53,794
And that 's all you can ask from a film , I think

1234
01:17:54,002 --> 01:17:58,048
Is you want a film to have a personality given to it

1235
01:17:58,257 --> 01:18:01,760
uniquely by its maker

1236
01:18:01,969 --> 01:18:05,013
that has something of the personality of the maker in it .

1237
01:18:09,601 --> 01:18:13,313
You know , I 'm even , these days , beginning to think

1238
01:18:13,522 --> 01:18:17,818
of an era in which I make movies , not only which , you know

1239
01:18:18,026 --> 01:18:19,778
you tend to hold closer to the vest by - -

1240
01:18:19,987 --> 01:18:22,072
Which is to say , you don 't show anyone the script

1241
01:18:22,281 --> 01:18:24,241
and you don 't ask anyone 's opinion .

1242
01:18:24,449 --> 01:18:27,661
But maybe even one day that you don 't even show the film to anyone .

1243
01:18:27,870 --> 01:18:31,248
Maybe you just make it and you look it , and then you say , " Okay . "

1244
01:18:31,456 --> 01:18:35,544
And then you put it in the closet drawer , and you forget it .

1245
01:18:39,172 --> 01:18:41,466
All you need to do that is a lot of money .

1246
01:18:50,601 --> 01:18:54,479
Clearly , I 've used parallel editing

1247
01:18:54,688 --> 01:18:59,151
and specifically in regard to a baptism in The Godfather .

1248
01:18:59,359 --> 01:19:05,282
Clearly , one of the tools you have in cinema is to juxtapose a ritual

1249
01:19:05,490 --> 01:19:09,077
a religious ritual of some type , as this is , a wedding

1250
01:19:09,286 --> 01:19:12,164
with other action happening at the same time .

1251
01:19:12,372 --> 01:19:15,542
And I 've done it before , and probably will do it again .

1252
01:19:15,751 --> 01:19:18,837
There 's a lot going on here , because there are these various killings

1253
01:19:19,046 --> 01:19:22,090
and awakening of Lucy .

1254
01:19:22,758 --> 01:19:27,804
So , yes , doing a collection of actions simultaneously happening

1255
01:19:28,013 --> 01:19:30,933
and edited parallel with a ritual

1256
01:19:31,141 --> 01:19:33,936
Is reminiscent of the time I did it first

1257
01:19:34,144 --> 01:19:37,147
which was in the baptism scene of The Godfather .

1258
01:19:38,649 --> 01:19:41,652
The orthodox religions , Greek Orthodox , Romanian Orthodox

1259
01:19:41,860 --> 01:19:45,280
Russian Orthodox , is , in fact , the original Christianity .

1260
01:19:45,489 --> 01:19:50,452
And for my part , I think the most beautiful expression of Christianity

1261
01:19:50,661 --> 01:19:52,913
that Roman Catholicism

1262
01:19:53,121 --> 01:19:57,000
Is Christianity having been fused with the Roman Empire

1263
01:19:57,209 --> 01:20:00,963
and , really , I think , has more to do with the Roman Empire

1264
01:20:01,171 --> 01:20:03,173
than it does with Christianity .

1265
01:20:03,382 --> 01:20:06,176
We 're shooting in a beautiful church here

1266
01:20:06,385 --> 01:20:10,055
in Los Angeles , a Greek Orthodox church .

1267
01:20:10,263 --> 01:20:12,891
We didn 't have this scene in the movie on the first shoot .

1268
01:20:13,100 --> 01:20:16,937
I forget - - I think we did it in some - - One of those stylized way

1269
01:20:17,145 --> 01:20:19,940
where you don 't really have anything but some shadows on the wall .

1270
01:20:20,148 --> 01:20:25,112
And looking at the film , I decided that having the real ceremony

1271
01:20:25,320 --> 01:20:28,907
the real wedding ceremony , as it might be in that religion

1272
01:20:29,116 --> 01:20:30,242
would be beautiful .

1273
01:20:30,450 --> 01:20:34,246
And this is pretty authentic , and , I think , very beautiful .

1274
01:20:36,289 --> 01:20:39,793
Because we actually did the ceremony , and we had the priest do the ceremony .

1275
01:20:40,002 --> 01:20:42,045
So in a sense , we realized when we were all done

1276
01:20:42,254 --> 01:20:45,382
that Keanu and Winona really are married

1277
01:20:45,590 --> 01:20:48,176
as a result of this scene and the ceremony .

1278
01:20:55,684 --> 01:20:58,145
And that 's clearly a homage

1279
01:20:58,353 --> 01:21:01,064
to Stanley Kubrick 's movie , The Shining .

1280
01:21:03,525 --> 01:21:07,237
I found the actual Romanian Orthodox churches

1281
01:21:07,446 --> 01:21:11,533
when I did go and spend time in Romania , which I did recently

1282
01:21:11,742 --> 01:21:14,911
making Youth Without Youth , very beautiful .

1283
01:21:15,120 --> 01:21:18,290
Maybe it 's because I wasn 't raised in it , and I 'm able to see it

1284
01:21:18,498 --> 01:21:22,294
as an expression of spiritual feelings

1285
01:21:22,502 --> 01:21:27,799
in terms of Christianity in a form that I don 't find oppressive and mean

1286
01:21:28,008 --> 01:21:33,138
which is how my childhood impression of religion was .

1287
01:21:38,977 --> 01:21:45,192
And this - - If I am talking about homage , this is Snow White in her glass coffin .

1288
01:21:45,400 --> 01:21:48,653
As a child , I just loved Snow White

1289
01:21:48,862 --> 01:21:51,448
because she had beautiful black hair , like my mother .

1290
01:21:51,656 --> 01:21:54,868
And I never could forget the glass coffin that she was in

1291
01:21:55,077 --> 01:21:57,162
and the seven dwarves mourning her .

1292
01:21:57,370 --> 01:21:59,664
That 's what this scene sort of looks like to me .

1293
01:21:59,873 --> 01:22:01,208
How can I believe ?

1294
01:22:01,416 --> 01:22:05,087
I want you to bring me , before nightfall , a set of post-mortem knives .

1295
01:22:05,295 --> 01:22:07,255
An autopsy ? Lucy ?

1296
01:22:07,464 --> 01:22:08,590
No , no , no , not exactly .

1297
01:22:08,799 --> 01:22:10,675
I just want to cut off her head and take out her heart .

1298
01:22:10,884 --> 01:22:12,636
Of course , when you make movies , you know

1299
01:22:12,844 --> 01:22:16,264
you tend to shoot in order of the set that 's up .

1300
01:22:16,473 --> 01:22:19,768
So that scene , the funeral scene , was shot way early in the movie

1301
01:22:19,976 --> 01:22:23,563
because we were shooting all the scenes in that mansion first .

1302
01:22:23,772 --> 01:22:27,359
And then way , way , way , later , these other scenes .

1303
01:22:27,567 --> 01:22:28,985
Streets of London .

1304
01:22:29,194 --> 01:22:33,365
For me , now that Lucy is dead , it is a sad homecoming .

1305
01:22:34,491 --> 01:22:36,993
It is as if a part of me is dead too

1306
01:22:37,202 --> 01:22:39,955
except for the tiny hope that lives in me

1307
01:22:40,163 --> 01:22:42,582
that I will again see my prince .

1308
01:22:42,791 --> 01:22:44,626
Is he here ?

1309
01:22:44,835 --> 01:22:46,294
Now that I am married

1310
01:22:46,503 --> 01:22:50,507
I begin to understand the nature of my feelings for my strange friend

1311
01:22:50,715 --> 01:22:52,050
who is always in my thoughts .

1312
01:22:52,259 --> 01:22:53,760
I don 't remember where this set was .

1313
01:22:53,969 --> 01:22:55,887
I know it was on some stage or another

1314
01:22:56,096 --> 01:23:00,016
but it 's a pretty - - I mean , this film - - Given that everything is built

1315
01:23:00,225 --> 01:23:05,522
with the exception of the Greek Orthodox church

1316
01:23:05,730 --> 01:23:08,233
which was a real church

1317
01:23:08,441 --> 01:23:12,362
there was a lot of work and production and sets building .

1318
01:23:12,571 --> 01:23:15,740
And the movie was not that costly a movie , as I recall .

1319
01:23:15,949 --> 01:23:19,744
I mean , nowadays , you hear of these movies that cost

1320
01:23:19,953 --> 01:23:22,539
you know , $ 80 million or $ 100 million .

1321
01:23:24,082 --> 01:23:28,670
This movie was far less than that , as I recall .

1322
01:23:28,879 --> 01:23:32,340
But when you look at it , you realize that everything is a set .

1323
01:23:32,549 --> 01:23:37,846
And all these people and costumes , and the time to do this stuff ...

1324
01:23:39,222 --> 01:23:41,266
I think we did it pretty economically

1325
01:23:41,474 --> 01:23:47,564
to put a stylish production on film for whatever it was .

1326
01:23:50,817 --> 01:23:54,196
I must confess , and this maybe sounds disheartening

1327
01:23:54,404 --> 01:23:57,866
but when I look at this and I think of , you know

1328
01:23:58,074 --> 01:24:01,203
all the work it is to make a movie , any movie

1329
01:24:01,411 --> 01:24:04,331
I have to say that unless it 's a theme

1330
01:24:04,539 --> 01:24:07,292
or a subject matter that you have to make

1331
01:24:07,500 --> 01:24:10,962
because it says something that has never been said before

1332
01:24:11,171 --> 01:24:14,549
or it just is in your soul , and you have to get it out

1333
01:24:14,758 --> 01:24:18,136
can 't see any point to wanting to make a film at all .

1334
01:24:18,345 --> 01:24:22,265
The way it 's been set up , and the way the whole profession has gone

1335
01:24:22,474 --> 01:24:24,601
it 's like you have to tolerate so much stuff .

1336
01:24:24,809 --> 01:24:28,230
You have to - - You work on this movie for so long

1337
01:24:28,438 --> 01:24:34,778
under such unenlightened directives from the company financing it .

1338
01:24:34,986 --> 01:24:40,533
And when it 's all said and done , they publish in the newspaper

1339
01:24:40,742 --> 01:24:43,411
like sports scores , how much money it did .

1340
01:24:43,620 --> 01:24:48,083
And they show it in a theater that 's a box with 10 other theaters .

1341
01:24:48,291 --> 01:24:50,627
And you have to , you know

1342
01:24:50,835 --> 01:24:54,130
not only hear the battery of critics

1343
01:24:54,339 --> 01:24:57,634
that rightly or wrongly say their opinion

1344
01:24:57,842 --> 01:25:00,345
but , like , absolutely everybody else .

1345
01:25:00,553 --> 01:25:03,014
It seems to me that the only reason to make a movie

1346
01:25:03,223 --> 01:25:06,184
Is because it 's something that 's never been made before

1347
01:25:06,393 --> 01:25:10,146
and it is really part of your feelings about life .

1348
01:25:10,355 --> 01:25:14,192
And therefore , it should be something

1349
01:25:14,401 --> 01:25:17,737
that you should finance as well as make .

1350
01:25:17,946 --> 01:25:22,993
Because that 's the only way that you can have the same right

1351
01:25:23,201 --> 01:25:25,287
that a painter has when he paints a picture

1352
01:25:25,495 --> 01:25:27,872
or a poet has when he writes a poem

1353
01:25:28,081 --> 01:25:33,211
or , to a large extent , a novelist has when he writes a novel .

1354
01:25:33,420 --> 01:25:37,507
Even , really , an opera composer , more or less

1355
01:25:37,716 --> 01:25:41,261
has the right to sit down and do his opera the way he sees it .

1356
01:25:41,469 --> 01:25:44,556
Of course , I suppose , to really , you know , produce Aida

1357
01:25:44,764 --> 01:25:47,017
you have to have someone pay for the elephants .

1358
01:25:47,225 --> 01:25:50,812
But you don 't hear so much about composers complaining

1359
01:25:51,021 --> 01:25:54,941
because the Bay of Egypt didn 't like the third act .

1360
01:25:55,150 --> 01:25:58,611
And what - - Of course , then , he was Giuseppe Verdi

1361
01:25:59,237 --> 01:26:01,114
So he didn 't have to put up with it .

1362
01:26:04,034 --> 01:26:06,536
A little child - - I mean , these poor kids .

1363
01:26:06,745 --> 01:26:09,956
I mean , the infant that I wanna meet , again , someday was just a little infant .

1364
01:26:10,165 --> 01:26:14,085
And we held her with great tenderness and affection .

1365
01:26:14,294 --> 01:26:16,963
But this little kid who was in this scene

1366
01:26:17,172 --> 01:26:20,133
had to be in the midst of all these obscenities

1367
01:26:20,342 --> 01:26:25,930
and , well , she was scared of the fake teeth and Lucy .

1368
01:26:26,139 --> 01:26:30,352
And can you imagine what a little 2-year-old

1369
01:26:30,560 --> 01:26:33,229
thinks of something like that ? It 's - -

1370
01:26:33,438 --> 01:26:35,815
We had a very difficult time calming her down

1371
01:26:36,024 --> 01:26:38,860
and getting her to even be present on the set .

1372
01:26:39,778 --> 01:26:43,281
Kiss me and caress me

1373
01:26:43,490 --> 01:26:45,742
my darling husband , please .

1374
01:26:45,950 --> 01:26:48,578
We wave Christ and His holy blood !

1375
01:26:49,621 --> 01:26:50,747
This scene

1376
01:26:50,955 --> 01:26:54,125
Is very much influenced by Cocteau , and ...

1377
01:26:54,334 --> 01:26:56,044
The candles lighting themselves .

1378
01:26:56,252 --> 01:26:58,338
And , of course , this scene was all acted backwards .

1379
01:26:58,546 --> 01:27:02,342
She was in the coffin , and started out in it , and got out .

1380
01:27:02,550 --> 01:27:05,261
And then we just - - We reversed it .

1381
01:27:10,392 --> 01:27:14,521
Winona Ryder was really thrilled with the cast .

1382
01:27:14,729 --> 01:27:16,606
I mean , she loved Gary Oldman

1383
01:27:16,815 --> 01:27:20,902
of course , Anthony Hopkins , and Richard E. Grant was one of - -

1384
01:27:21,111 --> 01:27:25,240
All of the actors . It was sort of her dream cast . And it was her idea .

1385
01:27:25,448 --> 01:27:26,950
She 's the one who gave me the script .

1386
01:27:27,158 --> 01:27:32,038
And in a way , I was very , very attentive to her wishes

1387
01:27:32,247 --> 01:27:34,707
and looked into all the actors she loved .

1388
01:27:37,419 --> 01:27:39,170
But she had a friend , Keanu

1389
01:27:39,379 --> 01:27:41,131
who was , you know , like , just a friend

1390
01:27:41,339 --> 01:27:45,427
and she liked very much , and thought was a very good person

1391
01:27:45,635 --> 01:27:46,678
and I did too .

1392
01:27:46,886 --> 01:27:50,598
And so we cast Keanu in the role .

1393
01:27:51,808 --> 01:27:53,643
But it wasn 't really my film , in a sense .

1394
01:27:53,852 --> 01:27:56,938
I mean , I was brought in , I was the director and what have you .

1395
01:27:57,147 --> 01:27:59,566
But , you know , it had been developed by other people .

1396
01:27:59,774 --> 01:28:02,235
You see a whole bunch of other producers and stuff on it .

1397
01:28:02,444 --> 01:28:05,613
And , you know , as I said , I was very fond of Keanu .

1398
01:28:05,822 --> 01:28:08,241
I thought he was a , you know , really nice kid .

1399
01:28:10,160 --> 01:28:11,661
Was she in great pain ?

1400
01:28:11,870 --> 01:28:14,456
Ja , she was in great pain . Then we cut off her head

1401
01:28:14,664 --> 01:28:17,792
and drove a stake through her heart and burned it and then she found peace .

1402
01:28:18,001 --> 01:28:21,296
Doctor ! Please .

1403
01:28:24,799 --> 01:28:26,968
So , Mr. Harker

1404
01:28:27,177 --> 01:28:29,345
I must now ask you , as your doctor ...

1405
01:28:29,554 --> 01:28:31,347
You know , from my standpoint

1406
01:28:31,556 --> 01:28:33,308
was doing this movie

1407
01:28:33,516 --> 01:28:35,435
as I said , on a string of movies that - -

1408
01:28:35,643 --> 01:28:37,896
I planned to do a few movies to get myself on my feet

1409
01:28:38,104 --> 01:28:40,190
and then I wasn 't gonna do these movies .

1410
01:28:40,398 --> 01:28:41,733
I wasn 't gonna do movies anymore .

1411
01:28:41,941 --> 01:28:44,569
I was gonna just do little personal things

1412
01:28:44,777 --> 01:28:46,571
which is more or less what I 've done .

1413
01:28:48,907 --> 01:28:52,160
- No ? - No .

1414
01:28:54,287 --> 01:28:55,330
Good !

1415
01:28:55,538 --> 01:28:57,999
Then you have not infected your blood with the terrible

1416
01:28:58,208 --> 01:29:00,585
disease that destroyed poor Lucy .

1417
01:29:09,135 --> 01:29:11,763
Doctor , you must understand .

1418
01:29:13,431 --> 01:29:15,808
I doubted everything

1419
01:29:16,017 --> 01:29:17,560
even my mind .

1420
01:29:17,769 --> 01:29:19,771
I was impotent with fear .

1421
01:29:19,979 --> 01:29:22,732
- I know . - But , sir

1422
01:29:22,941 --> 01:29:26,653
I know where the bastard sleeps .

1423
01:29:26,861 --> 01:29:30,532
I brought him there , to Carfax Abbey .

1424
01:29:33,284 --> 01:29:36,871
Vampires do exist .

1425
01:29:37,080 --> 01:29:40,041
And this one we fight , this one we face

1426
01:29:40,250 --> 01:29:42,126
has the strength of 20 or more people ...

1427
01:29:42,335 --> 01:29:44,295
I think if people really sit down

1428
01:29:44,504 --> 01:29:46,214
and read the Bram Stoker novel

1429
01:29:46,422 --> 01:29:52,428
they 'll find that this film follows the intent and the spirit of the book

1430
01:29:52,637 --> 01:29:54,097
closer than many .

1431
01:29:54,305 --> 01:29:58,893
With the one exception of the love story

1432
01:29:59,102 --> 01:30:02,355
which , of course , is the framing device , or the motivating device

1433
01:30:02,564 --> 01:30:04,440
at the beginning .

1434
01:30:04,649 --> 01:30:07,277
And as I say , was pretty much created by Jim Hart

1435
01:30:07,485 --> 01:30:12,657
in his original screenplay , which existed far before I did .

1436
01:30:12,865 --> 01:30:16,452
I mean , I was brought in as a director to do that script .

1437
01:30:16,661 --> 01:30:19,664
You 'll notice I have no screenwriting credit on this film

1438
01:30:19,872 --> 01:30:23,042
and that 's because , you know , beyond working as a director

1439
01:30:23,251 --> 01:30:24,919
making director 's suggestions

1440
01:30:25,128 --> 01:30:27,630
took no part in the writing of the screenplay .

1441
01:30:27,839 --> 01:30:29,299
planning on getting that close , doc .

1442
01:30:30,842 --> 01:30:34,596
You know , at the time , I thought well , I would do two movies .

1443
01:30:34,804 --> 01:30:38,766
I would do this film , Bram Stoker 's Dracula

1444
01:30:38,975 --> 01:30:41,269
and then I would do Mary Shelley 's Frankenstein

1445
01:30:41,477 --> 01:30:44,606
because I had a whole thought of how that should be done .

1446
01:30:44,814 --> 01:30:46,357
And then at the last minute , I said :

1447
01:30:46,566 --> 01:30:48,651
" What am I wasting my time for ?

1448
01:30:48,860 --> 01:30:51,487
I should be reading books or something . "

1449
01:30:51,696 --> 01:30:57,368
And we , of course , turned over that project to Kenneth Branagh

1450
01:30:57,577 --> 01:31:01,247
and , you know , was involved as a producer .

1451
01:31:01,456 --> 01:31:04,834
Of course , a producer , especially if the producer is a director

1452
01:31:05,043 --> 01:31:08,630
you have little to say to the new directors

1453
01:31:08,838 --> 01:31:11,341
because you don 't wanna come off as though , well

1454
01:31:11,549 --> 01:31:13,343
you 're some know-it-all director

1455
01:31:13,551 --> 01:31:15,511
who 's now gonna produce some other guy 's work .

1456
01:31:15,720 --> 01:31:19,724
I gave my notes to Kenneth Branagh on his version of Frankenstein

1457
01:31:19,932 --> 01:31:22,769
and ultimately he ...

1458
01:31:23,728 --> 01:31:29,400
You know , my main suggestion was they cut the first 25 minutes off of it

1459
01:31:29,609 --> 01:31:34,447
and get right to the story , which was the creation of the monster .

1460
01:31:34,656 --> 01:31:37,241
But if you see that movie , and you started a little later

1461
01:31:37,450 --> 01:31:40,912
a little bit more closer to the time he creates the monster

1462
01:31:41,120 --> 01:31:44,082
it has some interesting things in it .

1463
01:31:44,290 --> 01:31:45,708
That he is coming .

1464
01:31:47,001 --> 01:31:49,087
That he is coming for you .

1465
01:31:50,129 --> 01:31:52,173
Oh , please .

1466
01:31:58,262 --> 01:32:00,264
Don 't stay here .

1467
01:32:00,682 --> 01:32:05,311
Get away from these men , please .

1468
01:32:06,688 --> 01:32:10,608
And I pray to God I may never see your sweet face again .

1469
01:32:14,278 --> 01:32:16,906
And may the Lord bless and keep you .

1470
01:32:17,990 --> 01:32:19,117
Such a shame

1471
01:32:19,325 --> 01:32:21,411
that when they show these movies on television

1472
01:32:21,619 --> 01:32:24,080
